# Regression 1: Least Squares

Least squares fits a line, a plane or a hyperplane to data by making the sum of squared residuals as small as possible. It is the foundation of regression, and `ols_fit` reports with it everything needed to judge the fit: the uncertainty of each coefficient, the goodness of fit, diagnostics of the residuals, and warnings when the design is unreliable. After this chapter you can fit a model, read every field of the result and check it by hand, predict with intervals, control the options, deal with missing values and collinear predictors, and know which inputs are rejected.

Every program is complete, and its output is the output of that program.

## The calls

```cpp
reg::OLSFitResult reg::ols_fit(const Series &y, const Series &x, const reg::OLSOptions &options = {}, Config cfg = {});
Series            reg::ols_predict(const reg::OLSFitResult &fit, const Series &x_new, Config cfg = {});
std::string       reg::ols_summary(const reg::OLSFitResult &fit);
std::ostream     &reg::print_ols_summary(std::ostream &os, const reg::OLSFitResult &fit);
```

`y` has shape `[n]` and the predictors `x` have shape `[n, p]`; [Shapes and inputs](regression_01_least_squares/shapes_and_inputs.md) lists the accepted shorthand. By default an intercept column is added, and its coefficient comes first, named `const`. A model with no predictors and an intercept (`x` of shape `[n, 0]`) fits the sample mean.

| `OLSOptions` | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `alpha` | the intervals have coverage `1 − alpha` | 0.05 |
| `add_intercept` | add a column of ones | true |
| `drop_missing` | drop rows with a non-finite value; `false` makes them an error | true |
| `weights` | precision weights (finite, non-negative; zero removes the row) | none |
| `rcond` | relative cutoff below which a direction of the design counts as lost | 1e-12 |
| `response_name`, `predictor_names` | labels used by the summary and `coefficient_names` | `"y"`, none |
| `covariance` | the covariance estimator; `Nonrobust` is the only one | `Nonrobust` |

| `OLSFitResult` | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `coefficients`, `std_errors`, `t_values`, `p_values` | one per parameter |
| `conf_int` | `[k, 2]` lower and upper limits |
| `covariance` | the `[k, k]` covariance matrix of the coefficients |
| `fitted_values`, `residuals` | `[n]`, for the rows used |
| `nobs`, `rank`, `k_params`, `num_predictors`, `df_model`, `df_resid`, `dropped_observations` | counts |
| `rsquared`, `adj_rsquared`, `f_stat`, `f_pvalue` | goodness of fit |
| `sse`, `ssr`, `sst`, `mse`, `rmse` | sums of squares and the residual scale |
| `log_likelihood`, `aic`, `bic` | information criteria |
| `durbin_watson`, `jarque_bera`, `omnibus` (each with a p-value where defined), `skewness`, `kurtosis` | residual diagnostics |
| `condition_number`, `smallest_eig`, `warnings` | conditioning of the design |
| `has_intercept`, `covariance_type`, `response_name`, `coefficient_names`, `alpha` | what the fit was made with |

