## Checklist

1. `y` is `[n]`, `x` is `[n, p]`; the intercept is added and listed first. A row vector `[1, p]` is read as a column.
2. Read the standard errors and intervals, not just the coefficients; combine coefficients through `covariance`.
3. Check `warnings`, `rank` and `condition_number` before trusting a coefficient.
4. Without an intercept, R² and F are measured about zero and cannot be compared with an intercept model's.
5. Durbin–Watson is meaningful only when the rows are in time order.
6. Predict inside the range of the data; the intervals cannot warn of extrapolation.
7. Incomplete rows are dropped by default; read `dropped_observations`.

